  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current optimization strategy is simulated annealing, which is effective for global optimization but can be slow to converge to the optimal solution. The new approach uses Bayesian optimization with automatic Bayesian tuning (ABO) to efficiently explore the function space and find a better solution. Bayesian optimization balances exploration and exploitation by maintaining a probabilistic model of the objective function. The key improvements are:\n1. Using Gaussian Process (GP) regression to model the objective function\n2. Bayesian optimization to select queries adaptively\n3. Automatic Bayesian tuning to adjust hyperparameters dynamically\n4. Repair operation to maintain integral constraint\n\nThis method is expected to improve the solution by more effectively exploring the search space and leveraging the function's structure. The central idea is to replace the uniform local search with an adaptive global optimization method that learns the function's behavior and focuses queries in promising regions.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Gaussian Process setup\n- Use scikit-learn for GP regression\n- Handle the integral constraint implicitly via repair\n- Prepare training data with initial knowledge\n2. Bayesian optimization loop\n- Initialize with provided parent solution and random perturbations\n- In each iteration:\n  a. Query the GP to predict best next point\n  b. Evaluate new candidate via repair and objective calculation\n  c. Update GP model with new data point\n3. Repair operation implementation\n- Maintain integral constraint via scaling/clipping\n4. Early termination based on convergence criteria\n5. Final candidate evaluation\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- Base GP model with Mat\u00e9rn kernel\n- Initial training data size: 10 points (current best from parent)\n- Expected improvement acquisition function\n- Thompson sampling for balance between exploration and exploitation\n- Automatic parameters: \u03b1 (balance parameter)\n- Bounds: [0,1] for each h[i]\n- Repair function slope parameter: 0.1\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness\n- Time budget: 100 seconds\n- Precompute training data out-of-time slices\n- Backup repair operations for numerical stability\n- Stop if improvement plateaued\n- PC-ADAM solver for stochastic optimization\n- Early termination of GP if model confidence exceeds threshold\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n- Repair final candidate to ensure feasibility\n- Re-evaluate final solution using correlation\n- Check integral constraint (sum(h) \u2248 140 for n_points=280)\n- Return tuple (h_values, C5_bound, n_points)\n- All search operations work with n_points=280 parent length\n\nThe method leverages probabilistic modeling to efficiently navigate the constrained optimization space. The Gaussian Process provides a balance between exploration and exploitation while the repair mechanism ensures that the solution remains feasible throughout the optimization process.",
